[BLAME] benchmark_5/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: The latest CHANGE note addressed error-path dispatch binding for invalid graph states, but it did not extend behavioral coverage of the public contract. Uncovered requirements include removing a node that still has dependents, where incident-edge cleanup must also detach the removed identifier from downstream prerequisite sets; ordering a graph whose deferred dependency check must surface a missing-prerequisite error; computing the longest weighted path on an empty graph; succeeding a node while an already-terminal dependent must remain terminal; continuing after an error while other pending nodes with satisfied prerequisites are promoted to ready; and reflecting scheduler state after a registered node is removed. Completion checks also lack pending nodes with a succeeded prerequisite alongside a running prerequisite, and pending nodes blocked by an already-failed prerequisite, including multiple blocked dependents sharing that prerequisite. Add unit cases exercising these public behaviors so every contracted branch is reached.
[BLAME] benchmark_5/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: The CHANGE note describes deferred dependency validation and failure-policy handling, yet five statements remain unreachable under the specified contract. The downstream guard that skips identifiers absent from the in-degree map is impossible because every registered downstream identifier is a node; the cycle-walk guard that skips prerequisites absent from the traversal color map is impossible because missing prerequisites are rejected before cycle detection; the revisiting guard in completion analysis is only reachable for a cyclic graph, which the DAG contract forbids and validate()/run() reject first; and the runner's empty-wave break plus its non-ready wave-member guard cover scheduler states the contracted execution flow never produces. Restructure to remove these dead branches, or annotate each with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: <reason>)' where the branch encodes an impossible state or a caller assumption guaranteed by the contract.
[CHANGE] benchmark_5/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Resolve unreachable-branch feedback: removed the impossible downstream in-degree guard and missing-color cycle-walk guard (both endpoints are validated/registered before these walks), and dropped the cyclic-graph revisiting guard from completion analysis since the contract forbids cycles and validate()/run() reject them first; annotated the runner's defensive empty-wave break and non-ready wave-member guard with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: ...)' explaining the acyclic, unmutated-DAG invariant; also pruned the analogous redundant 'p in earliest' critical-path filters and the redundant 'other_id != node_id' FAIL_FAST condition. Behavior re-verified against contracts for topological ordering, cycles, missing dependencies, policies, retries, and empty graphs.
[CHANGE] benchmark_5/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Extended behavioral coverage of the public contract. Updated the scheduler model so non-FAIL_FAST mark_failed policies promote unblocked PENDING nodes whose prerequisites all succeeded, and allowed compute_critical_path on an empty graph. Added fake unit cases for: removing a node with dependents (detaching downstream prerequisite edges), topological_sort surfacing a deferred missing-prerequisite error, empty-graph critical path, mark_succeeded leaving an already-terminal dependent terminal, CONTINUE_ON_ERROR promotion of satisfied pending nodes, scheduler state after node removal, is_finished with a succeeded+running prerequisite mix, and multiple dependents blocked by a failed prerequisite. Added matching real-implementation tests for the same scenarios so every contracted branch is exercised.
